From 1664dd3ba3f6a593f9e5e7163b42c570b614c11e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: J P <jpwatters@mac.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 16:43:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/42] pi-relay: run the QMX relay on a Raspberry Pi 4/5 as a
 systemd service

tools/pi-relay installs the same relay (tools/nas-relay/qmx_nas_relay.py,
unchanged) on a Raspberry Pi with the QMX plugged into it:

- qmx-relay.timer starts qmx-relay.service 60 s after every boot.
- Restart=on-failure, 10 s apart, at most 3 restarts (StartLimitBurst=4 over
  365d); a 4th failure is final and qmx-relay-gaveup.service logs GAVE UP.
- qmx-relay-event.sh (ExecStartPre/ExecStopPost/boot/OnFailure) writes each
  boot, start (timer, restart n of 3, by hand) and stop (cause, restart or
  not) to /var/log/qmx-relay/qmx-relay.log next to the relay's own output;
  logrotate weekly.
- qmx-relay-ctl: status, log, follow, test, probe, restart, stop, start.
- udev rule: ModemManager and PipeWire/PulseAudio leave the QMX alone, no
  USB autosuspend. install-pi.sh / uninstall-pi.sh.

No Pi-specific relay is needed: its ctypes usbfs structures and ioctl numbers
match <linux/usbdevice_fs.h> on aarch64 exactly.

QMX-Panadapter-Shared.pdf: chapters 17-20 (Pi relay, sources, driver
listings, application listings); date in the cover table replaced, not
overlaid.

Tested: installer/uninstaller run with systemctl/udevadm stubbed (two bugs
fixed), systemd-analyze verify, logrotate -d, shellcheck, relay started with
the unit's command line (--fake): SELFTEST and NETTEST PASSED, Find NAS
answered; every log-hook scenario. Not yet run under systemd on a real Pi.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
